      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-307",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Missing rate limiting on login endpoint  [CVSS 9.1: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "app/routes/session.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 53
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "cvssRating": "Critical",
            "category": "CWE-307",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-307",
            "cweId": "CWE-307",
            "cvssScore": 9.1,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe handleLoginRequest function directly processes login attempts from req.body without any rate limiting, account lockout, CAPTCHA, or delay mechanism. No middleware or logic restricts the number of attempts per IP address, user account, or time window, allowing exhaustive password guessing.\n\n#### Impact\nAn unauthenticated attacker can send unlimited login attempts without any throttling, enabling brute-force and credential-stuffing attacks against user accounts. Successful guessing leads to unauthorized access to accounts and their associated PII.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker sends repeated POST requests to /login with a known username (e.g., admin) and different passwords from a wordlist. Because there is no lockout or rate limiting, the attacker can try thousands of passwords per minute until the correct one is found.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has network access to the login page\n- Attacker knows a valid username (or can enumerate usernames via error messages)\n\n```\nthis.handleLoginRequest = (req, res, next) => {\n    const { userName, password } = req.body;\n    userDAO.validateLogin(userName, password, (err, user) => {\n        ...\n    });\n};\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nImplement rate limiting on the login route using a middleware like express-rate-limit, or add account lockout after N consecutive failed attempts. The check should be placed before the call to userDAO.validateLogin on line 58.\n\n**Exploitability:** Missing rate limiting on login endpoint allows unlimited brute-force attempts against user credentials. Combined with weak passwords (6) and username enumeration (5/7) yields full account takeover.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 No rate limiting, account lockout, CAPTCHA, or delay exists anywhere in the login flow; `POST /login` is directly exposed without middleware; distinct error messages additionally enable username enumeration to accelerate brute-force attacks."
          },
          "taxa": [
            {
              "toolComponent": {
                "name": "CWE",
                "guid": "b7c8d9e0-1f2a-3b4c-5d6e-7f8090a1b2c3"
              },
              "id": "CWE-307"
            }
          ],
          "rank": 91.0,
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "vvaFindingId/v1": "vvaf1_5812a403e68d98ab40c5439b0929680e"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Server uses HTTP exposing sensitive data in cleartext  [CVSS 9.1: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "server.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 145
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "cvssRating": "Critical",
            "category": "CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-319 - Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information",
            "cweId": "CWE-319",
            "cweName": "Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information",
            "cvssScore": 9.1,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe server is started with http.createServer(app) at line 145, with HTTPS alternative commented out. The application handles authentication and sensitive user data (SSN, bank accounts) that will be transmitted in plaintext over the network.\n\n#### Impact\nAll communication between the client and server is unencrypted, allowing network eavesdroppers to intercept sensitive data such as login credentials, session cookies, and PII. This can lead to mass credential theft and session hijacking.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker on the same WiFi network as a user performs a man-in-the-middle attack and captures the session cookie from an HTTP request. The attacker then replays the cookie to impersonate the user and access their private data.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has network position to sniff traffic\n- Victim accesses the application over HTTP\n\n```\nhttp.createServer(app).listen(port, () => {\n    console.log(`Express http server listening on port ${port}`);\n});\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nEnable the HTTPS server using the certificates and set up proper TLS. Use `https.createServer` instead of `http.createServer`, and redirect HTTP to HTTPS.\n\n**Exploitability:** Server uses plain HTTP; credentials, session tokens, and all sensitive data are transmitted in cleartext, enabling passive eavesdropping.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 HTTP server with no TLS; session cookies, passwords, SSN, bank details all in cleartext; no upstream proxy termination."
          },
          "taxa": [
            {
              "toolComponent": {
                "name": "CWE",
                "guid": "b7c8d9e0-1f2a-3b4c-5d6e-7f8090a1b2c3"
              },
              "id": "CWE-319"
            }
          ],
          "rank": 91.0,
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "vvaFindingId/v1": "vvaf1_d9725ede73a299754c5fd85f554bbb6c"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-943",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "NoSQL Injection in Login via UserName  [CVSS 9.8: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "app/data/user-dao.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 92
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "security-severity": "9.8",
            "cvssRating": "Critical",
            "category": "CWE-943",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-943",
            "cweId": "CWE-943",
            "cvssScore": 9.8,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.93,
            "description": "#### Description\nIn session.js handleLoginRequest (line 55), `userName` is extracted from `req.body` and passed to `userDAO.validateLogin()`. Inside user-dao.js (line 92), that value is used directly in `usersCol.findOne({ userName: userName })` without any type checking or sanitization. If `userName` is an object (e.g., from a JSON\u2011encoded request), MongoDB interprets it as a query operator, allowing the attacker to match any user in the collection. The password comparison then uses the plaintext password from the matched user; an attacker who knows a common plaintext password can log in as that user without knowing the username.\n\n#### Impact\nThe login handler passes the user\u2011supplied userName directly into a MongoDB `findOne` query without sanitization. An attacker can send a JSON object as `userName` containing MongoDB operators (e.g., `{\"$gt\": \"\"}`) to bypass the username lookup, causing the query to match an arbitrary user. When combined with the known plaintext password of that user, this allows full authentication bypass without knowing the target username.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nSend a POST request to `/login` with `Content-Type: application/json` and body `{\"userName\": {\"$gt\": \"\"}, \"password\": \"user1\"}`. The query `{ userName: { \"$gt\": \"\" } }` matches any user with a string userName. The first result (e.g., the admin account) is returned. If the attacker knows the password for that account (e.g., the default password 'user1' for the default 'user1' account), the comparison succeeds and the attacker is logged in as that user\u2014gaining admin privileges if the matched user is an administrator.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must know or guess at least one plaintext password of any user (trivial due to weak passwords and plaintext storage)\n- Application accepts JSON\u2011encoded request bodies (default for Express body\u2011parser)\n\n```\nusersCol.findOne({\n    userName: userName\n}, validateUserDoc);\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nSanitize all query parameters to ensure they are strings before using them in MongoDB queries. Use `$eq` explicitly or type\u2011check inputs: `usersCol.findOne({ userName: { $eq: String(userName) } })` or validate that `userName` is a string early in `handleLoginRequest`.\n\n**Exploitability:** NoSQL injection in login allows bypassing authentication without knowing a username. Exploitable pre-auth by sending a crafted JSON body, immediately granting a session as an arbitrary user.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 userName flows from req.body through bodyParser.json() directly into usersCol.findOne() with no typeof check, sanitization, or schema validation; MongoDB native driver interprets object payloads as query operators; confirmed route is unauthenticated POST /login with no middleware defenses."
          },
          "taxa": [
