          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "security-severity": "9.9",
            "cvssRating": "Critical",
            "category": "CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-306 - Missing Authentication for Critical Function",
            "cweId": "CWE-306",
            "cweName": "Missing Authentication for Critical Function",
            "cvssScore": 9.9,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.85,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe docker-compose.yml sets the connection string to `mongodb://mongo:27017/nodegoat` without credentials, and the mongo service does not define any authentication environment variables (e.g., MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME). As a result, the database starts with no access controls, allowing any process on the Docker network to read/write all data.\n\n#### Impact\nThe MongoDB instance is configured without any authentication. An attacker who gains code execution inside the web container can directly access and exfiltrate all stored data, including user passwords, SSNs, and other PII, without needing additional credentials.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nA remote attacker exploits the Server-Side JavaScript Injection (eval) in contributions to execute arbitrary commands. From the container, they use the mongo client to connect to mongo:27017 with no authentication. They can then dump the entire nodegoat database, including hashed (or plaintext) passwords and personally identifiable information.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- The attacker must achieve code execution inside the web container (e.g., via the known eval injection).\n\n```\nMONGODB_URI: mongodb://mongo:27017/nodegoat\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nSet up MongoDB authentication by adding environment variables to the mongo service in docker-compose.yml: MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME and MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD. Update the MONGODB_URI to include the credentials, e.g., mongodb://admin:[REDACTED-URL-CRED]@mongo:27017/nodegoat?authSource=admin.\n\n**Exploitability:** MongoDB started with no authentication; any network-level actor inside the Docker network can read/write the entire database. Exploitable via SSRF (17) from an authenticated session.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 docker-compose.yml configures MongoDB with zero authentication; the mongo service lacks MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME/--auth; an attacker who gains RCE via the eval() injection in contributions (reachable from open signup) can connect from the web container to mongo:27017 without credentials and dump/modify all data, including plaintext passwords and PII."
          },
