          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "7.7",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:N/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-918 - Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)",
            "cweId": "CWE-918",
            "cweName": "Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)",
            "cvssScore": 7.7,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\ndisplayResearch builds the outbound URL by directly concatenating attacker-controlled query parameters req.query.url and req.query.symbol with no allow-list, scheme validation, or private-address blocking. It passes this URL to needle.get, making the server fetch the attacker-chosen target and return the body in the response.\n\n#### Impact\nAny authenticated user can force the server to make GET requests to arbitrary URLs, including localhost, internal services, and cloud metadata endpoints. This can expose internal data or cloud IAM credentials and enable lateral movement inside the network.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAuthenticate as any low-privilege user, then request /research?url=http://localhost:4000/&symbol=admin to reach an internal admin page. On AWS, a request such as /research?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/&symbol=<role-name> can retrieve temporary IAM credentials. The server concatenates and fetches the URL, and the response body is written back to the client.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Valid application user account and login session\n- Backend has network reachability to the target host\n\n```\nif (req.query.symbol) {\n    const url = req.query.url + req.query.symbol;\n    return needle.get(url, (error, newResponse, body) => {\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nReplace user-controlled URL fetching with a fixed stock-data client that only calls an allow-listed host. If dynamic fetching remains necessary, validate the scheme, block loopback/RFC1918/link-local addresses, and do not return raw fetched bodies to the browser.\n\n**Exploitability:** Authenticated SSRF via research endpoint. Attacker can reach internal services (e.g., unauthenticated MongoDB) and read responses. Post-auth, low privilege.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 Authenticated SSRF with no allow-list, scheme validation, or private-address blocking; any logged-in user can force the server to fetch arbitrary URLs and receive the response body."
          },
