          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "8.2",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-1104: Use of Unmaintained Third Party Components",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-1104 - Use of Unmaintained Third Party Components",
            "cweId": "CWE-1104",
            "cweName": "Use of Unmaintained Third Party Components",
            "cvssScore": 8.2,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe docker-compose.yml defines the mongo service using `image: mongo:4.4`. MongoDB 4.4 is unmaintained, leaving any deployed database vulnerable to all publicly known CVEs for that version. The image is not pinned by digest, adding further supply-chain risk.\n\n#### Impact\nThe MongoDB 4.4 community edition image reached end-of-life in February 2024 and no longer receives critical security patches. An attacker who gains access to the internal Docker network can compromise the database via known, unpatched MongoDB vulnerabilities, leading to data loss or exposure.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAfter gaining a foothold inside the web container (e.g., via eval injection in contributions), an attacker uses the local MongoDB client to connect to the mongo service and exploits a known privilege-escalation or authentication-bypass flaw in MongoDB 4.4 to dump all databases.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- An attacker must first gain some degree of access to the internal Docker network (e.g., by compromising the web container or exploiting an SSRF that can speak the MongoDB wire protocol).\n- A known vulnerability in MongoDB 4.4 must be exploitable from the attacker's position.\n\n```\nimage: mongo:4.4\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nUpgrade to a supported MongoDB image (e.g., mongo:7.0 or mongo:8.0) and pin with a digest. Enable authentication by setting MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME and MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD environment variables in docker-compose.yml.\n\n**Exploitability:** Docker Compose uses end-of-life MongoDB 4.4; vulnerable to all known unpatched CVEs for that version.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 8/10) \u2014 `docker-compose.yml` line 14 uses `image: mongo:4.4`, an EOL MongoDB version with no authentication configured. The web container (same Docker network) has confirmed exploitable SSJS injection (`eval()` at `app/routes/contributions.js:32-34`), SSRF, and NoSQL injection \u2014 providing a real pivot path to the unmaintained database. No digest pinning, no `MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_*` credentials, and `expose` (not `ports`) offers only Docker-network isolation, which the compromised web container bypasses."
          },
