            "confidence": 0.9,
            "description": "*1 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\nThe package-lock.json resolves the devDependency `grunt-if` to the mutable URL `https://github.com/binarymist/grunt-if/tarball/master`. When `npm install` is run, npm downloads the current contents of the master branch and executes package installation scripts. There is no integrity hash or fixed version pin to protect against changes to the tarball. An attacker with commit access to that branch (or who can take over the account) can modify the package to include malicious install scripts, which will run on any developer or CI machine that installs the project's dependencies.\n\n#### Impact\nA remote attacker who can alter the GitHub repository branch 'master' for the grunt-if project can execute arbitrary code on any machine that runs `npm install` from this repository. This compromises the developer's workstation and any CI/CD build environment used by the project.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker pushes malicious code to the `binarymist/grunt-if` repository's master branch. When a developer or CI server runs `npm install`, npm fetches the modified tarball, and the attacker's code executes with the privileges of the installing user, allowing exfiltration of credentials, source code, or deployment credentials.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- The repository's master branch is compromised or the attacker has write access to the GitHub repository\n- A developer or CI/CD system runs npm install after the malicious commit\n\n```\n\"grunt-if\": \"https://github.com/binarymist/grunt-if/tarball/master\",\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nReplace the mutable tarball URL with a fixed version and integrity hash, e.g., use a pinned release version or commit hash with package-lock integrity verification, or vendor the required code locally.\n\n**Exploitability:** Grunt-if dependency fetched from an unpinned GitHub tarball. An attacker with commit access to the branch could inject malicious install scripts, leading to developer/CI compromise.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 8/10) \u2014 Mutable tarball URL resolves to master branch; integrity hash protects `npm ci` but `npm install` silently accepts changes; upstream repo compromise leads to code execution on developer machines.",
