            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "6.8",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-384: Session Fixation",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-384 - Session Fixation",
            "cweId": "CWE-384",
            "cweName": "Session Fixation",
            "cvssScore": 6.8,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "*1 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\nIn handleLoginRequest at line 116, `req.session.userId = user._id` is executed without first calling `req.session.regenerate()`. The comment block (lines 104-118) explicitly acknowledges this vulnerability and provides a fix (regenerate the session). By not regenerating the session, an attacker who learns or sets a session cookie with a known ID before the user logs in can later present that cookie and gain full access as the victim.\n\n#### Impact\nAfter a successful login, the same session ID is used without regeneration. If an attacker can fixate a session identifier before the victim logs in (e.g., by setting a cookie via a phishing link or man-in-the-middle), they can hijack the victim's authenticated session and access the victim's account, including all personal data and admin functions if the victim is an admin.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\n1. Attacker sends victim a link that sets a cookie 'connect.sid=s:fixedvalue' (e.g., via a session fixation vulnerability in a subdomain or an HTTP response splitting attack).\n2. Victim clicks the link, the cookie is set in their browser, then they log in to the application.\n3. The server assigns 'fixedvalue' as the victim's authenticated session ID (since it doesn't regenerate).\n4. Attacker now sends requests with the same cookie 'connect.sid=s:fixedvalue' and is recognized as the logged-in victim, gaining full access to the victim's dashboard, allocations, etc.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must be able to set a cookie in the victim's browser for the application domain (e.g., via cookie injection, MITM, or cross-site scripting on a subdomain).\n- Session store trusts the cookie value without regeneration.\n\n```\nreq.session.userId = user._id;\nreturn res.redirect(user.isAdmin ? \"/benefits\" : \"/dashboard\");\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nRegenerate the session immediately after successful authentication, before setting the userId. Replace line 116 with `req.session.regenerate(() => { req.session.userId = user._id; return res.redirect(...); })` as suggested in the comment.\n\n**Exploitability:** Session fixation: missing session regeneration on login. Attacker can pre-set a known session cookie, then victim logs in, attacker hijacks. Requires user interaction.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 `handleLoginRequest` at line 116 assigns `userId` to the session without calling `regenerate()`; the session ID set before login persists post-authentication, enabling session fixation. The explicit commented fix confirms the vulnerability is known.",
            "dedupRelatedLocationCount": 1
          },
