        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-614",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Session cookie lacks security attributes  [CVSS 6.4: CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "server.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 77
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "6.4",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-614",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-614",
            "cweId": "CWE-614",
            "cvssScore": 6.4,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *AV:A (non-network)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe express-session middleware is configured in server.js (lines 77-102) without the 'httpOnly', 'secure', or 'sameSite' cookie flags. The application listens on plain HTTP (line 145), so session cookies are transmitted in cleartext. Without 'httpOnly', client-side scripts can access the cookie if XSS is present. Without 'secure', the cookie is sent even over unencrypted requests.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker on the same network can intercept the session cookie via passive eavesdropping or active MITM on HTTP connections. This allows full account takeover for any authenticated victim.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker performs ARP spoofing on a local network to intercept HTTP traffic. A victim logs in to the application, and the session cookie 'connect.sid' is captured in cleartext. The attacker uses this cookie to impersonate the victim and access their profile, allocations, and memos.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must be able to intercept network traffic (same LAN, open WiFi, or compromised router)\n- Victim must authenticate over HTTP\n\n```\napp.use(session({\n    secret: [REDACTED-SECRET],\n    saveUninitialized: true,\n    resave: true\n}));\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nIn server.js lines 95-99, uncomment and extend the cookie configuration: set `cookie: { httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'strict' }`. Also deploy HTTPS (uncommented lines 152-155) to enforce 'secure' cookies.\n\n**Exploitability:** Session cookie lacks httpOnly, secure, and sameSite flags. Combined with XSS, cookies can be stolen and reused.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 session cookie lacks `secure` and `sameSite` flags; transmitted in cleartext over HTTP. Scanner incorrectly claimed `httpOnly` is missing but the core vulnerability stands."
          },
