            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-639 - Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key",
            "cweId": "CWE-639",
            "cweName": "Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key",
            "cvssScore": 4.3,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.9,
            "description": "*1 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\nThe route /allocations/:userId is protected by isLoggedIn middleware but does not enforce that req.session.userId matches the route parameter req.params.userId. The displayAllocations handler (in allocations.js) uses req.params.userId directly to query the database, allowing horizontal privilege escalation. An attacker authenticated as user A can request /allocations/userB to see user B's financial allocations.\n\n#### Impact\nAny authenticated user can view allocation data (including sensitive financial PII) of other users by changing the :userId URL parameter. No authorization check verifies that the requesting user matches the requested userId.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAuthenticated attacker with userId '12345' browses to /allocations/67890 and views another user's bank account numbers, balances, and allocation history without any additional authorization.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must be authenticated with any valid user account\n\n```\napp.get(\"/allocations/:userId\", isLoggedIn, allocationsHandler.displayAllocations);\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nIn the displayAllocations handler, verify that req.params.userId matches req.session.userId before querying the database. Reject requests where the IDs do not match, or use the session userId exclusively to fetch data.\n\n**Exploitability:** IDOR in allocations: any authenticated user can read allocations of other users by changing userId URL parameter. Post-auth.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 `displayAllocations` uses `req.params.userId` directly with no ownership check against `req.session.userId`; only `isLoggedIn` middleware guards the route, which does not prevent horizontal access; the commented-out fix confirms the vulnerability is known.",
            "dedupRelatedLocationCount": 1
          },
