          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-117",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-117",
            "cweId": "CWE-117",
            "cvssScore": 5.3,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe handleLoginRequest handler logs the raw userName directly on line 64 without any sanitization or encoding. The userName is taken from req.body (untrusted network input). Attackers can supply a username containing newline characters (CRLF) to inject arbitrary log lines or control characters to disrupt log analysis. Comments on lines 66-80 even acknowledge this vulnerability and suggest fixes, but the actual code remains vulnerable.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker can inject CRLF sequences and ANSI control characters into server logs by providing a specially crafted username during login. This can be used to forge log entries, obfuscate malicious activity, or exploit downstream log processors (e.g., Log Injection to RCE in vulnerable log viewers).\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker submits a login request with a username like 'attacker%0d%0aINJECTED: admin login from 10.0.0.99%0d%0a'. The server logs this unescaped input, causing a forged log entry to appear in the log stream. Subsequent log analysis tools or alerting systems may be misled about the origin of other events.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker can send HTTP requests to the application's login endpoint\n\n```\nconsole.log(\"Error: attempt to login with invalid user: \", userName);\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nSanitize log output by stripping or encoding control characters from user-supplied strings before logging. Replace the direct interpolation on line 64 with a safe logging format (e.g., console.log('Error: %s', safeUserName)) or encode newlines/CRLF as shown in the existing comments (lines 66-80).\n\n**Exploitability:** Log injection via CRLF in login username. Pre-auth, can disrupt log integrity but no direct exploit chain.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 Untrusted req.body.userName flows unsanitized into console.log on an unauthenticated POST /login route; CRLF injection can forge log entries in downstream log aggregation/analysis systems."
          },
