            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 1.0,
            "description": "*23 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\nIn handleSignup, when validation fails, the errors object containing unescaped userName and email is rendered in the signup template without encoding. With autoescape false, this results in XSS. Similar vulnerabilities exist in the duplicate-user and dashboard success renders.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker can execute arbitrary JavaScript in a victim's browser by getting them to submit a crafted signup request, leading to credential theft or account compromise.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker hosts a page that sends a POST to /signup with userName='<script>alert(1)</script>' and a mismatched password. The response renders the script, executing it in the victim's browser.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Victim submits a crafted POST to /signup\n- autoescape false\n\n```\nreturn res.render(\"signup\", {\n    ...errors,\n    environmentalScripts\n});\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nEscape all user-controlled data before rendering templates. In the signup route, apply HTML encoding to userName, email, etc., or enable Swig autoescaping.\n\n**Exploitability:** Reflected XSS in signup error due to autoescape=false. Pre-auth, requires user click. Combined with missing httpOnly, can steal cookies.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 user-controlled userName from req.body flows unsanitized into res.render with Swig autoescape:false; rendered in HTML attribute context without encoding on unauthenticated POST /signup",
            "dedupRelatedLocationCount": 23
          },
