          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-352 - Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)",
            "cweId": "CWE-352",
            "cweName": "Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)",
            "cvssScore": 6.5,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.75,
            "description": "*1 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\nhandleProfileUpdate is a state-changing POST endpoint that persists all profile fields using the session userId at line 82. The handler never validates a CSRF token or same-origin signal. Since the session cookie is sent automatically with cross-site form POSTs, the update can be initiated by an attacker's page without user consent.\n\n#### Impact\nA malicious website can silently submit a profile update using a victim's existing session, modifying their stored SSN, date of birth, bank account, address, and routing number. This violates profile integrity and can be chained with stored XSS.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker creates a page containing an auto-submitting form to /profile with arbitrary profile fields. A logged-in victim visits the page; the browser attaches their session cookie and submits the update, changing the victim's bankAccount and address.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Victim is logged into the application\n- Victim visits an attacker-controlled page or clicks a link\n\n```\nprofile.updateUser(\n    parseInt(userId),\n    firstName,\n    lastName,\n    ssn,\n    dob,\n    address,\n    bankAcc,\n    bankRouting,\n    (err, user) => {\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nApply CSRF token validation to mutating routes. In app/routes/profile.js, reject requests at or before line 82 unless a valid per-session CSRF token accompanies the request; additionally set SameSite=Lax or Strict on the session cookie.\n\n**Exploitability:** Missing CSRF protection on profile update. Attackers can craft cross-site requests to modify user profiles. Post-auth, requires victim interaction.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 CSRF middleware (csurf) is commented out in server.js; no sameSite cookie, no origin/referer check, no token validation on the /profile POST handler; attacker can forge cross-site requests that modify victim's bank account, SSN, and PII via session cookie replay.",
            "dedupRelatedLocationCount": 1
          },
