            "confidence": 0.85,
            "description": "*1 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\ndisplayAllocations destructures threshold from req.query with no validation and passes it directly to allocationsDAO.getByUserIdAndThreshold(userId, threshold, ...). Inside that DAO method the threshold value is concatenated into a Mongo $where JavaScript expression. Since the value is not coerced to a number or allow-listed, an attacker can break out of the expression and alter the query logic.\n\n#### Impact\nAn authenticated attacker can manipulate the threshold parameter to inject JavaScript into the Mongo $where clause, likely gaining access to all allocation records regardless of the userId and potentially causing server-side code execution inside the database query.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn authenticated user requests GET /allocations/victim123?threshold=0;return true;//. The value is interpolated into the $where expression, causing MongoDB to execute the injected JavaScript and return allocations the user should not see.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has any authenticated session\n- threshold parameter is accepted as a string and interpolated into $where\n\n```\nconst { threshold } = req.query;\n\nallocationsDAO.getByUserIdAndThreshold(userId, threshold, (err, allocations) => {\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nValidate or coerce threshold to a number in displayAllocations or in the DAO, and replace $where string interpolation with a parameterized MongoDB query operator.\n\n**Exploitability:** NoSQL injection via threshold parameter in allocations. Authenticated user can extract arbitrary data. Post-auth.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 Full call chain verified from Express route \u2192 handler \u2192 DAO \u2192 $where interpolation; no validation anywhere; fix intentionally commented out; any authenticated user can exploit.",
