          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-209: Generation of Error Message Containing Sensitive Information",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-209 - Generation of Error Message Containing Sensitive Information",
            "cweId": "CWE-209",
            "cweName": "Generation of Error Message Containing Sensitive Information",
            "cvssScore": 5.3,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe global Express error-handling middleware at app/routes/error.js receives unauthenticated requests (any route error bubbles here). It passes the raw 'err' object directly into res.render()'s template context without sanitization or filtering. The Swig template engine (configured with autoescape: false) renders the error object's properties (message, stack, and any custom fields) into the HTML response. An attacker can send a malicious request (e.g., NoSQL injection, malformed JSON, or triggering a database validation error) and read the full error disclosure in the rendered page source.\n\n#### Impact\nAn unauthenticated attacker triggering any application error receives the complete error object in the rendered HTML response. This can expose stack traces, internal file paths, database query details, library versions, and sensitive logic, enabling targeted further attacks.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker sends a GET request to /allocations/1?threshold=malformed (triggering a known NoSQL injection error) or submits a profile update with an invalid SSN. The application crashes into this error handler, which renders a page containing the full error object stack trace, MongoDB query, and internal file paths. The attacker views the page source to extract this information.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker can send any HTTP request that triggers an error in any route handler (no authentication required for most error paths)\n\n```\nres.status(500);\nres.render(\"error-template\", {\n    error: err\n});\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nIn app/routes/error.js, never pass the raw error object to the template. Either render a static generic error page (no error data), or construct a safe context object with only a user-friendly message. For development, log the full error server-side only.\n\n**Exploitability:** Error handler leaks full error object to client, including stack traces. Pre-auth, aids reconnaissance.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 Unauthenticated POST /login and POST /signup routes bubble DB errors via next(err) to errorHandler, which passes the raw err object to a Swig template with autoescape:false; no NODE_ENV guard or sanitization exists."
          },
