            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe package.json pins marked to version 0.3.5 (line 17). This version predates the marked v0.4.0 security fix and does not sanitize HTML by default. When combined with the application's global Swig autoescape=false setting, user-supplied Markdown in memos passes through marked unsanitized and is rendered with injected scripts. The data flow: attacker sends a memo via POST /memos (memos.js::addMemos) with body containing malicious Markdown; the memo is stored in MongoDB; any user viewing the memo (memos.js::displayMemos) receives the unsanitized HTML output, executing the XSS.\n\n#### Impact\nAttackers can inject arbitrary HTML/JavaScript into memos which will execute in the browsers of all users viewing the memo, leading to session hijacking and credential theft.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn authenticated attacker posts a memo containing '[click](javascript:alert(document.cookie))'. When any user views the memo, the JavaScript executes in their browser, leaking their session cookie to the attacker.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has a valid authenticated session\n- Victim views the attacker's memo\n\n```\n\"marked\": \"0.3.5\"\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nUpgrade marked to version 4.0.0 or later which sanitizes HTML by default, or configure a sanitizer like DOMPurify on the server-side before rendering.\n\n**Exploitability:** Outdated marked library (0.3.5) renders Markdown unsanitized. Combined with Swig autoescape=false enables stored XSS. Requires authenticated memo creation.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 marked@0.3.5 sanitize mode has documented bypasses; autoescape:false removes defense-in-depth; authenticated attacker \u2192 stored XSS confirmed"
          },
