            "confidence": 0.85,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe validateLogin function compares the provided password with the stored password using a plain == operator. This comparison short-circuits on the first mismatching character, leaking timing information that an attacker can use to determine the password character by character.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker can exploit timing differences in the password comparison to iteratively guess the correct password of any user, leading to unauthorized account access.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker sends a series of login requests for a target username, each with a guessed password. By measuring the response time differences, they can deduce which characters are correct. Over multiple requests, they reconstruct the full password and gain access to the account.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must be able to send multiple login requests and measure response times with sufficient accuracy.\n\n```\nconst comparePassword = [REDACTED-SECRET], fromUser) => {\n  return fromDB === fromUser;\n};\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nUse a constant-time comparison function, such as `crypto.timingSafeEqual` in Node.js, to compare passwords or hashes. Additionally, hash passwords with a secure algorithm like bcrypt before storage.\n\n**Exploitability:** Non-constant-time password comparison leaks password length via timing. Practically low risk, but could aid offline attacks.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 `POST /login` is unauthenticated and directly feeds `req.body.password` into `comparePassword()` which uses non-constant-time `===`; bcrypt constant-time comparison is commented out; no rate limiting, lockout, or any other compensating control exists."
          },
